INT. CLOCKMAKER'S SHOP - NIGHT

Hundreds of clocks, all ticking, none in agreement. Brass gears under glass domes. Pendulums swing in long cases along the walls. A single green-shaded lamp lights a workbench strewn with tiny screwdrivers.

INSPECTOR LOUISA HARDCASTLE (50s, wool overcoat, gray streak in her hair) studies a grandfather clock whose glass door hangs open. Behind the bench, the shop's owner, BERNADETTE OKAFOR-LISTER (50s, leather apron, loupe pushed up on her forehead), watches her with her hands flat on the wood.

LOUISA
You said the clock stopped at 3:15.

BERNADETTE
It did.

LOUISA
And the man was found at 3:17.

BERNADETTE
Yes.

LOUISA
Then he was killed at 3:15.

BERNADETTE
Or the clock was wrong.

LOUISA
Your clocks are never wrong, Ms. Okafor-Lister.

BERNADETTE
Mine, no. His, perhaps.

LOUISA
Whose?

BERNADETTE
The victim's. He wound it himself.

LOUISA
He, who?

BERNADETTE
The man who died.

LOUISA
You said he came to you for repairs.

BERNADETTE
He did. He said it ran slow.

LOUISA
And did it?

BERNADETTE
Not when I looked.

She runs her gloved finger along the clock's pendulum, then lifts it to the lamp. A thin dusting of gold powder. She sniffs it.

LOUISA (CONT'D)
Gold leaf.

BERNADETTE
It would be. For the dial.

LOUISA
It's on the pendulum, not the dial.

She does not answer. A hundred clocks tick on. A cuckoo bursts from a case in the dark and sings once, shrilly, and withdraws.

LOUISA (CONT'D)
Ms. Okafor-Lister. Who else had a key?

BERNADETTE
Only the man himself.

LOUISA
And the man's widow.

BERNADETTE
I didn't say that.

LOUISA
No. I did.

She looks at Louisa. Louisa looks at her. Then, slowly, she reaches under the workbench and brings up a small velvet pouch, tilting it so that the gold dust trickles onto the bench.

BERNADETTE
He told me it was for the restoration.

LOUISA
And you believed him.

BERNADETTE
She did.

Louisa closes her eyes. The clocks strike the half hour, all at different times.
